## Run

```bash
composer install
cp .env.example .env                              # then edit APP_SECRET
php bin/console doctrine:schema:update --force    # create the schema from the entities
symfony serve                                     # or: php -S localhost:8000 -t public
```

### Schema & migrations

There is **no** Backbone-generated SQL migration: Doctrine owns the schema. For local
development `doctrine:schema:update --force` diffs your entities against the database and
applies the changes in place. **For production, use real migrations instead:**

```bash
composer require doctrine/doctrine-migrations-bundle
php bin/console make:migration
php bin/console doctrine:migrations:migrate
```

## Layout

```
public/index.php            # front controller — YOURS (written once, never overwritten)
bin/console                 # Symfony console — YOURS
src/Kernel.php              # minimal kernel (FrameworkBundle + DoctrineBundle) — YOURS
config/packages/            # framework.yaml, doctrine.yaml — YOURS
config/routes.yaml          # imports attribute routes from src/Generated/Controller — YOURS
src/Generated/              # owned by Backbone — regenerated wholesale, DO NOT EDIT
  Entity/                   # Doctrine entities (#[ORM\Entity], columns, relations, get/set)
  Repository/               # ServiceEntityRepository per entity
  Controller/               # AbstractController REST controllers (attribute routes)
<% if (auth) { %>  Security/TokenManager.php  # signs / verifies the HMAC auth token
<% } %>```

Everything under `src/Generated/` is overwritten every time you regenerate. Everything else is
yours: add bundles in `src/Kernel.php`, config in `config/`, and your own controllers/services
anywhere outside `src/Generated/`.

